**Description:** Whitelisting in the `Staking` and `StakingWithUnbonding` contracts is implemented using Merkle proof validation. The leaf `node` is generated in the `stake()` function if whitelisting is enabled using the address of the `_receiver` and the `merkleIndex` and `merkleAmount` parameters:

```
bytes32 node = keccak256(
    abi.encodePacked(merkleIndex, _receiver, merkleAmount)
);

if (!MerkleProof.verifyCalldata(merkleProof, MERKLE_ROOT, node))
    revert StakingErrors.InvalidMerkleProof();
```

However, the `merkleIndex` and `merkleAmount` parameters are unused in the contracts, and including these parameters in the leaf node generation costs more than only using the `_receiver` address.

It is also important to note that the gas cost of Merkle proof validation varies based on tree size. Larger trees require larger Merkle proofs, and therefore, a very large tree can significantly increase the gas costs of the whitelisting validation.

**Recommendation:** Consider thoroughly testing the gas costs associated with Merkle proof validation to ensure they remain within acceptable limits. If gas usage proves to be a concern, switching to a more efficient whitelisting mechanism, such as using a hashmap or an external contract, could significantly reduce costs.

## Severity

Critical Risk

## Description

When uninstalling the module, there is no authentication made to the `msg.sender`, the sender parameter is extracted from the data, and we deal with it as if it is the actual sender.

This will allow anyone to call `CredibleAccountModule` directly and `onInstall()` the module (validator/Hook) from any wallet.

## Location of Affected Code

File: [src/modules/validators/CredibleAccountModule.sol#L327](https://github.com/etherspot/etherspot-modular-accounts/blob/d4774db9f544cc6f69000c55e97627f93fe7242b/src/modules/validators/CredibleAccountModule.sol#L327)

```solidity
function onUninstall(bytes calldata data) external override {
    if (data.length < 32) {
        revert CredibleAccountModule_InvalidOnUnInstallData(msg.sender);
    }
    uint256 moduleType;
    address sender;
    assembly {
        moduleType := calldataload(data.offset)
>>      sender := calldataload(add(data.offset, 32))
    }
    bytes memory uninstallData = data[32:];
    ...
}
```

## Impact

Anyone can uninstall a module from any wallet.

## Recommendation

It should be ensured that the `sender` is the same as `msg.sender`.

